Usages:
  di_resolution: |
    The injector resolves dependencies per `Module`, walking constructor and property
    metadata attached by decorators, and caches resolved instances keyed by a ContextId
    (a static context for singletons, a fresh context per inbound request for
    REQUEST-scoped providers). A provider not found in the current module is looked up
    in the module's imports, stopping at the first module that both provides and
    exports the token.

Annotations: |
  This cell is Nest's dependency-injection container: it owns the module graph
  (`NestContainer`, `Module`), the per-class dependency-resolution algorithm
  (`Injector`), the per-instance metadata/state holder (`InstanceWrapper`), and the
  bootstrap step that turns the graph into live instances (`InstanceLoader`).
  Use `di_resolution` wherever a method walks the provider/injectable/controller
  dependency graph.

---

"NestContainer(applicationConfig: any, contextOptions: Object<string, any>)":
  location: container.ts
  annotations: |
    Root registry of the application's module graph. One instance is created per
    Nest application and is shared by every subsystem (router, microservices,
    websockets) that needs to look up a module, a provider, or the active HTTP adapter.

    `applicationConfig`: global config (global pipes/guards/filters/interceptors,
    versioning)
    `contextOptions`: options such as moduleIdGeneratorAlgorithm and snapshot

    Algorithm:
    1. On construction, select a module-opaque-key factory (deep-hash or by-reference)
       used to compute stable identity tokens for dynamic modules
    2. addModule compiles the module metatype into a token via the module compiler;
       if a module with that token is already registered, return the existing `Module`
       instead of creating a duplicate
    3. A freshly created `Module` is marked global (and given maximum distance, so its
       lifecycle hooks always run first) when @Global() metadata or dynamic
       global: true is present
  properties:
    "serializedGraph -> Object<string, any>": |
      Graph representation used by introspection tooling (e.g. application preview) to
      inspect the module graph without instantiating providers.
  methods:
    "addModule(metatype: Object<string, any>, scope: Array<Object<string, any>>) -> result: Promise<Object<string, any>>": |
      Register a module class (or dynamic module) in the container.

      `metatype`: a module class, a dynamic module descriptor, or a Promise of one
      `scope`: the chain of importing modules, used for circular-import diagnostics

      Algorithm:
      1. Reject an undefined `metatype` (guards against a forward reference that
         resolves to nothing)
      2. Compile it to a token via the module compiler
      3. If that token is already registered, return the existing module unchanged
      4. Otherwise create a new `Module`, register its dynamic metadata and imports
         recursively, and return it as newly inserted
    "addProvider(provider: Object<string, any>, token: string, enhancerSubtype: string) -> key: any": |
      Register a provider under the module identified by `token`, then notify the
      discoverable-metadata registry so @Discoverable consumers can see it.
    "addInjectable(injectable: Object<string, any>, token: string, enhancerSubtype: string, host: Object<string, any>) -> wrapper: InstanceWrapper": |
      Register a guard/pipe/interceptor/filter class as an injectable on the module
      identified by `token`, optionally scoped to a specific `host` class.
    "addController(controller: Object<string, any>, token: string)": |
      Register a controller class on the module identified by `token` and assign it a
      unique internal id used for graph inspection.
    "getModules() -> modules: Object<string, Module>": |
      Return the live map of all registered modules, keyed by module token.
    "getHttpAdapterRef() -> adapter: any": |
      Return the HTTP adapter instance bound to this container via setHttpAdapter,
      used by the router and lifecycle code to reach the underlying HTTP server.
    "bindGlobalScope()": |
      For every registered module, import every module previously marked global so
      providers exported by global modules are reachable everywhere.

"Module(metatype: Object<string, any>, container: NestContainer)":
  location: module.ts
  annotations: |
    Represents one @Module()-decorated class after compilation: its own providers,
    controllers, injectables (guards/pipes/interceptors/filters), imports and exports.
    Every provider/controller/injectable is wrapped in an `InstanceWrapper` before
    being stored, never held as a bare class reference.

    `metatype`: the module class this instance represents
    `container`: owning `NestContainer`, used to read shared application config

    Use `di_resolution` for how `Injector` walks this module's providers and imports.
  properties:
    "providers -> Object<string, InstanceWrapper>": |
      Providers declared or re-exported by this module, keyed by injection token.
    "injectables -> Object<string, InstanceWrapper>": |
      Guards, pipes, interceptors and exception filters registered on this module.
    "controllers -> Object<string, InstanceWrapper>": |
      Controllers declared by this module.
    "imports -> Array<Module>": |
      Other modules this module imports; searched by `Injector` when a token is not
      found locally.
    "exports -> Array<any>": |
      Tokens (providers or re-exported modules) this module makes available to
      importers.
    "isGlobal -> boolean": |
      When true, this module's exports are implicitly imported into every other
      module.
  methods:
    "addProvider(provider: Object<string, any>, enhancerSubtype: string) -> token: any": |
      Wrap `provider` in an `InstanceWrapper` and store it under its injection token,
      dispatching to class/value/factory/use-existing handling based on the provider's
      shape.
    "addInjectable(injectable: Object<string, any>, enhancerSubtype: string, host: Object<string, any>) -> wrapper: InstanceWrapper": |
      Wrap and store a guard/pipe/interceptor/filter class, attaching enhancer
      metadata to `host` (its owning controller or provider) when supplied.
    "addController(controller: Object<string, any>)": |
      Wrap and store a controller class, assigning it a unique controller id.
    "addExportedProviderOrModule(toExport: Object<string, any>)": |
      Add a token or re-exported module to this module's exports, validating that the
      token is either locally provided or one of this module's own imports.
    "hasProvider(token: any) -> found: boolean": |
      Whether a provider is registered locally under `token`.
    "getProviderByKey(token: any) -> wrapper: InstanceWrapper": |
      Look up the `InstanceWrapper` registered locally under `token`.
    "createModuleReferenceType() -> ref: Object<string, any>": |
      Build a module-reference class bound to this module, giving scoped get/
      resolve/create access to this module's providers.

"Injector(options: Object<string, any>)":
  location: injector.ts
  annotations: |
    Resolves and instantiates one `InstanceWrapper` at a time: reads constructor and
    property dependency metadata, recursively resolves each dependency through the
    owning `Module` (and its imports), then calls the target constructor or factory.

    `options`: preview (skip real instantiation), snapshot (deterministic ids),
    instanceDecorator (wrap every freshly created instance)

    Use `di_resolution` for the module-graph traversal this class performs.
  methods:
    "loadInstance(wrapper: InstanceWrapper, collection: Object<string, InstanceWrapper>, moduleRef: Module, resolutionContext: Object<string, any>) -> done: Promise<void>": |
      Resolve and construct the instance for `wrapper` if not already resolved or
      pending; concurrent resolution attempts for the same instance await the same
      pending result instead of instantiating twice.
    "loadProvider(wrapper: InstanceWrapper, moduleRef: Module, resolutionContext: Object<string, any>) -> done: Promise<void>": |
      loadInstance specialised for providers, followed by resolving that provider's
      own guards/pipes/interceptors/filters for the same request context.
    "loadController(wrapper: InstanceWrapper, moduleRef: Module, contextId: Object<string, any>) -> done: Promise<void>": |
      loadInstance specialised for controllers.
    "resolveConstructorParams(wrapper: InstanceWrapper, moduleRef: Module, inject: Array<any>, callback: Object<string, any>, resolutionContext: Object<string, any>, parentInquirer: InstanceWrapper) -> done: Promise<void>": |
      Resolve every constructor parameter of `wrapper` in parallel (each recursively
      resolved through lookupComponent), then invoke `callback` with the resolved
      argument list once all of them settle.

      Algorithm:
      1. Determine the dependency token list from class constructor metadata or, for
         factory providers, from the declared inject array
      2. For each dependency, resolve its owning `InstanceWrapper` in the current
         module or its imports, then recursively ensure that dependency is itself
         instantiated
      3. Once all dependencies resolve, invoke `callback` with the ordered instances
    "lookupComponent(providers: Object<string, InstanceWrapper>, moduleRef: Module, dependencyContext: Object<string, any>, wrapper: InstanceWrapper, resolutionContext: Object<string, any>) -> wrapper: Promise<InstanceWrapper>": |
      Find the `InstanceWrapper` for a dependency token, first locally on `moduleRef`,
      then in its imports; throws when a self-reference or an unresolved token is
      found.
    "instantiateClass(instances: Array<any>, wrapper: InstanceWrapper, targetMetatype: InstanceWrapper, resolutionContext: Object<string, any>) -> instance: Promise<any>": |
      Call the target class constructor (or factory function) with the resolved
      `instances`, apply the configured instance decorator, and mark the instance
      resolved for its ContextId.

"InstanceWrapper(metadata: Object<string, any>)":
  location: instance-wrapper.ts
  annotations: |
    Wraps one provider/controller/injectable class together with its DI metadata
    (token, scope, durability) and its instances, keyed per ContextId so that
    DEFAULT-scoped providers hold a single instance while REQUEST/TRANSIENT-scoped
    providers hold one instance per resolution context.
  properties:
    "token -> any": |
      Injection token this wrapper is registered under.
    "scope -> string": |
      DI scope: DEFAULT (singleton), REQUEST, or TRANSIENT.
    "metatype -> Object<string, any>": |
      The class (or factory function) this wrapper instantiates.
  methods:
    "getInstanceByContextId(contextId: Object<string, any>, inquirerId: string) -> host: Object<string, any>": |
      Return the per-context instance record (instance, resolved/pending flags) for
      `contextId`, creating an empty record on first access.
    "setInstanceByContextId(contextId: Object<string, any>, value: Object<string, any>, inquirerId: string)": |
      Store the resolved instance record for `contextId`.

"InstanceLoader(container: NestContainer, injector: Injector, graphInspector: Object<string, any>)":
  location: instance-loader.ts
  annotations: |
    Bootstraps every registered module: first creates bare prototypes for every
    provider/injectable/controller (so circular references can see each other's
    prototype before construction), then resolves and constructs the real instances.
  methods:
    "createInstancesOfDependencies(modules: Object<string, Module>) -> done: Promise<void>": |
      `modules`: defaults to every module registered on the owning `NestContainer`

      Algorithm:
      1. For every module, create prototypes for providers, injectables, then
         controllers
      2. For every module (in parallel), resolve real instances for providers,
         injectables, then controllers via `Injector`
      3. On failure, register the partial graph with the graph inspector before
         re-throwing, so an application preview can still report a partial graph

